    {
      "id": "Y3jegDnt_wNByJWdgpEyA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1535.2716770822356,
      "y": 72.28023859991941,
      "width": 824.6423705826081,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 526339248,
      "version": 8858,
      "versionNonce": 428534400,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1765507116213,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) L4 vs L7 Loadbalancers for web sockets: \n2) Purpose behind using chat participant table if we already have user entity \n    - We can do a composite primary key on the chatId and participantId fields. A\nrange lookup on the chatId will give us all participants for a given chat.\n    - We'll need a Global Secondary Index (GSI) with participantId as the partition\nkey and chatId as the sort key. This will allow us to efficiently query all chats for\na given user. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) L4 vs L7 Loadbalancers for web sockets: \n2) Purpose behind using chat participant table if we already have user entity \n    - We can do a composite primary key on the chatId and participantId fields. A range lookup on the chatId will give us all participants for a given chat.\n    - We'll need a Global Secondary Index (GSI) with participantId as the partition key and chatId as the sort key. This will allow us to efficiently query all chats for a given user. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
