    {
      "id": "rstz4feiULcvAVgkbvjeC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1540.8139010599102,
      "y": 334.87063420034076,
      "width": 836.2130886205714,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 852302464,
      "version": 1641,
      "versionNonce": 741575296,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1765512421272,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) 2B DAU -> expecting 200M of them to be connected at any one time. per host we\ncan server 1-2M users hence we will need hundreds of chat servers. \nNew Issues: If User A is trying to send a message to User B and C, but User B and\nC are connected to different Chat Servers, we're going to have a problem.\n- Bad Solution: Naively horizontally scale over load balancer. \n- Good Solution: Using consistent hashing to keep sticky sessions. \n    challenges: Increasing the number of Chat Servers requires careful orchestration\nof dropping connections so that users reconnect to other servers without triggering\na thundering herd (we need to be able to support users moving between servers).\nDuring scaling we need to ensure that events are sent to both servers to prevent\ndropping messages.\n- Great Solution: Using a pub-sub with chatId as the topic and various participants\nas the consumers. \n\n2) Handling multiple clients: single user multiple devices. Create client table. client id in\ninbox table. Instead of sending messages to a given participant, now we send\nmessages to certain participant clients. We will need to implement certain limit on the\nnumber of clients per user tho. \n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) 2B DAU -> expecting 200M of them to be connected at any one time. per host we can server 1-2M users hence we will need hundreds of chat servers. \nNew Issues: If User A is trying to send a message to User B and C, but User B and C are connected to different Chat Servers, we're going to have a problem.\n- Bad Solution: Naively horizontally scale over load balancer. \n- Good Solution: Using consistent hashing to keep sticky sessions. \n    challenges: Increasing the number of Chat Servers requires careful orchestration of dropping connections so that users reconnect to other servers without triggering a thundering herd (we need to be able to support users moving between servers). During scaling we need to ensure that events are sent to both servers to prevent dropping messages.\n- Great Solution: Using a pub-sub with chatId as the topic and various participants as the consumers. \n\n2) Handling multiple clients: single user multiple devices. Create client table. client id in inbox table. Instead of sending messages to a given participant, now we send messages to certain participant clients. We will need to implement certain limit on the number of clients per user tho. \n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
